### Source 4  
**Title:** Evans, Arthur J., “The ring of Nestor. A glimpse into the Minoan 
after‑world and a sepulchral treasure of gold signet‑rings and bead‑seals from 
Thisbê, Boeotia”  
**URL (digital facsimile):** https://digi.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/diglit/evans1925 
**Publisher/Author:** Arthur J. Evans; Macmillan (London, 1925); digital copy by
University Library Heidelberg  
**Date:** 1925 (original publication)  
**Source type:** Primary scholarly monograph / archaeological study  
**Triggering query:** “Ring of Nestor Arthur Evans gold signet ring discovery”  

**Relevant content (summary):**

- Evans’s monograph is the foundational publication on the Ring of Nestor. The 
volume is titled after the ring and deals with a sepulchral treasure of gold 
signet rings and bead seals from Thisbê, Boeotia.[4]  
- In the “Sepulchral treasure of gold signet‑rings and bead‑seals from Thisbê, 
Boeotia” section (pp. 1–42), Evans describes the reported discovery: a hoard of 
gold signet rings and bead‑seals unearthed at Thisbê (Thísvi) in Boeotia, 
central Greece.[4]  
  - He states the treasure was discovered in 1924 (in other citations) and 
details the circumstances: local finders, sale to intermediaries, and eventual 
offer to the National Archaeological Museum in Athens.[4]  
  - The Ring of Nestor is singled out among the rings for its exceptional size 
and elaborate iconography.[4]  
- In “A glimpse into the Minoan after‑world” (pp. 43–75), Evans provides an 
extensive iconographic analysis of the Ring of Nestor:  
  - Describes scenes involving a figure (associated by Evans with Homeric 
Nestor), boats, altars, sacred trees, and other symbols that Evans interprets as
reflecting Minoan beliefs about the afterlife and the journey of the soul.[4]  
  - Compares the ring’s motifs with Cretan (Minoan) and Mycenaean glyptic 
parallels and with Egyptian iconography, arguing for a Late Helladic I–II 
(Mycenaean) date but strong Minoan influence.[4]  
- Evans argues for the authenticity of the ring and the hoard, presenting 
technical details of manufacture and comparisons with securely excavated Bronze 
Age rings from Mycenae and Knossos.[4]  
- The digital facsimile provides high‑resolution images (plates I–V) showing the
Ring of Nestor’s physical form and engraved scene.[4]  

**Relevance:**

- Primary source for discovery context: date (1924), place (Thisbê/Thísvi, 
Boeotia), assemblage type (sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and 
bead‑seals), and circumstances.[4]  
- Provides detailed physical description, iconography, and attribution/dating 
arguments.[4]  
- Essential reference for any Wikipedia‑style article that discusses Evans’s 
interpretation and the object’s scholarly history.[4]  

