### Source 6  
**Title:** “Historic Images of the Greek Bronze Age” (Metropolitan Museum of Art
– Perspectives)  
**URL:** 
https://www.metmuseum.org/perspectives/historic-images-of-the-greek-bronze-age  
**Publisher/Author:** The Metropolitan Museum of Art; essay referencing Nanno 
Marinatos’s work  
**Date:** 17 May 2011  
**Source type:** Museum essay / institutional commentary  
**Triggering query:** ““Ring of Nestor” archaeological artifact discovery 
context”  

**Relevant content (summary):**

- Discusses historical photographs and reconstructions of Greek Bronze Age 
objects and sites, including the work of Émile Gilliéron fils, known for 
restorations and forgeries.[6]  
- Notes that scholar Nanno Marinatos has argued that the “Ring of Minos” and the
“Ring of Nestor” were forgeries by E. Gilliéron fils.[6]  
- Summarizes the impact of alleged forgeries on the interpretation of Minoan and
Mycenaean art, and the need for critical assessment of provenance and technical 
features.[6]  

**Relevance:**

- Shows that at least some modern scholarship (Marinatos) regards the Ring of 
Nestor as a forgery, reinforcing the existence of ongoing debate.[6]  
- As a major museum’s interpretive essay, it provides an institutional 
perspective on authenticity controversies.[6]  

