### Source 2  
**Title:** “No (e)scape? E‑scaping arguments and gemmae dubitandae in Aegean 
glyptic”  
**URL:** 
https://archiv.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/propylaeumdok/4408/1/Hudler_E-scaping%20argu
ments_2019.pdf  
**Publisher/Author:** Katharina Hudler; Propylaeum e-doc (University of 
Heidelberg repository)  
**Date:** 2019  
**Source type:** Academic article / scholarly repository PDF  
**Triggering query:** ““Ring of Nestor” archaeological artifact discovery 
context”  

**Relevant content (summary):**

- Examines “gemmae dubitandae” (doubtful glyptic objects) in Aegean archaeology,
explicitly mentioning the Ring of Nestor as a case study.[2]  
- Notes that objects such as the Ring of Nestor had long‑standing doubts 
regarding their archaeological properties and authenticity; these doubts arose 
because of problematic or undocumented provenance and suspicions of modern 
manufacture.[2]  
- Surveys prior scholarly arguments both for and against authenticity, 
referencing historical discussions and contextual data (such as alleged 
discovery in Boeotia, association with other gold signet rings and bead 
seals).[2]  
- Emphasizes that recent technological and iconographic studies have improved 
the understanding of such objects’ status, including the Ring of Nestor, by 
considering fabrication techniques, wear, stylistic parallels with securely 
excavated Aegean rings, and archival documentation.[2]  

**Relevance:**

- Confirms that the Ring of Nestor is treated as an archaeological artifact 
within the scholarly literature, though historically subject to doubts.[2]  
- Adds detail on the nature of scholarly debates surrounding authenticity and 
provenance.[2]  
- Supports notability by showing the ring is discussed in specialist Aegean 
glyptic research.[2]  

