  "documents": [
    {
      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "Arthur J. Evans’s 1925 monograph presents a detailed 
study of a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals reportedly 
found at Thisbê (Thísvi) in Boeotia, central Greece, with particular emphasis on
a large gold signet-rin g that he calls the \"Ring of Nestor\". The first part 
describes the discovery circumstances and funerary context of the treasure, 
situating it within Boeotian burial customs and the broader Middle to early Late
Bronze Age environment of the Greek mainland in relation to Minoan and early 
Mycenaean culture. Evans catalogues each ring and seal, giving weights, 
dimensions of the bezels, inner ring diameters, and describing their manufacture
and iconography, supported by photographic plates and line drawings by Émile 
Gilliéron fils. The \"Ring of Nestor\" is singled out as one of the most 
important pieces, characterized by a very large oval bezel engraved with a 
complex multi-figure scene. Evans analytically describes the scene as composed 
of several pictorial units: a central tree (later often interpreted as a world 
tree), a guardian animal by the tree which he variously calls a watch-dog or 
dragon, seated female or divine figures, ritual participants, altars (including 
incurved altars), animals and other symbols. In the second part he interprets 
these images as providing a glimpse into a Minoan conception of the afterlife, 
arguing that the ring reflects Minoan religious beliefs, rites and afterworld 
imagery transmitted into early Mycenaean elite funerary contexts. He attributes 
the treasure, including the ring, to the early phases of the Late Bronze Age 
(approximately 16th–15th centuries BCE) within the Minoan–Mycenaean cultural 
koine and defends the ring’s authenticity as an ancient artifact.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "A.G. Vlachopoulos’s 2020 chapter re-examines the 
\"Ring of Nestor\" (catalogued as CMS VI, no. 277) in the broader context of 
Aegean iconography and debates over the authenticity of certain gold signet 
rings, including the so-called Ring of Minos. The study reviews the history of 
scholarship from the 1920s onwards, highlighting how doubts about the ring’s 
authenticity were often grounded in the ambiguous and partly legendary nature of
its reported discovery circumstances rather than in systematic technical or 
iconographic analysis. Vlachopoulos presents new technical and comparative 
observations, including detailed measurements and a survey of carving style, to 
argue that the ring’s manufacture is consistent with securely provenanced Late 
Bronze Age Aegean gold rings. He reports that the bezel of the Ring of Nestor 
measures 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm, making it the fifth largest known Aegean signet 
ring, and compares it with other large gold rings. The chapter also offers a 
fresh iconographic interpretation of the bezel scene. Particular emphasis is 
placed on the animal guarding the central tree, previously described by Evans as
a dog or dragon; Vlachopoulos argues that it should instead be identified as a 
scorpion, a well-attested apotropaic motif in Bronze Age Aegean imagery. He 
proposes that the scene depicts a scorpion killing a Minoan dragon in front of a
world tree, integrating this with other elements such as altars and human 
figures to reconstruct a complex religious narrative. On chronological grounds, 
informed by stylistic and technological parallels, he places the ring in the 
first centuries of the Late Bronze Age (circa 16th–15th century BCE) within the 
Cretan–Mycenaean tradition and concludes that the ring is a genuine ancient 
artifact rather than a modern forgery.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "Theodore Eliopoulos’s 2013 article offers detailed 
observations on the iconography of the \"Ring of Nestor\" and addresses its 
place in longstanding debates about authenticity. The study foregrounds 
iconographic analysis as a key method for evaluating the ring in light of 
unresolved or conflicting results from technical examinations. Eliopoulos 
systematically examines elements such as the central tree, the incurved altars, 
the human figures, and the guardian animal, comparing them with motifs on other 
Aegean artifacts whose authenticity is not in question. He argues that features 
like the incurved altars correspond closely to documented Aegean 
representations, supporting the case that the ring’s imagery is consistent with 
genuine Late Bronze Age iconographic traditions. At the same time, he considers 
whether certain aspects of the composition might reflect early 20th-century 
cultural practices or aesthetic sensibilities, warning against over-reliance on 
perceived modern stylistic traits as evidence of forgery. The article situates 
the Ring of Nestor alongside the Ring of Minos as central case studies in the 
category of \"fake\" or doubtful Aegean rings, and emphasizes that the growth of
new discoveries and digital dissemination of images has complicated rigid 
divides between securely provenanced objects and those with incomplete 
provenance.",
      "source_ref": "s3"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d4",
      "document_summary": "The Wiley-published journal article titled \"The 
‘Ring of Nestor’\" presents new technical observations concerning the gold 
signet ring known by that name, with the aim of assessing its authenticity. 
