{"query": {"id": "20260628165353777129_e59941c3", "handle": "q2", "text": "“Ring
of Nestor” archaeological artifact discovery context", "importance": "core"}, 
"items": [{"id": "20260628165550269328_2251be09", "type": "Evidence", "text": 
"Vlachopoulos characterizes the Ring of Nestor as a large gold Aegean signet 
ring with dimensions of about 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm and notes that within the 
corpus of Aegean rings it ranks as the fifth largest known example.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628165547378908_0c84b63c"], "citations": [1]}, {"id": 
"20260628165550345843_acfdde0b", "type": "Evidence", "text": "In discussing 
provenance, Vlachopoulos reports that the Ring of Nestor is said to have formed 
part of a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals from Thisbê in
Boeotia, with this context cited from earlier studies by Evans and other 
scholars.", "source_ids": ["20260628165547378908_0c84b63c"], "citations": [1]}, 
{"id": "20260628165550422210_25dc05ab", "type": "Evidence", "text": 
"Vlachopoulos notes that past scholars had categorized the Ring of Nestor among 
gemmae dubitandae, but argues that recent analyses of manufacturing technique, 
iconography, and contextual documentation now support its authenticity as a Late
Helladic artifact.", "source_ids": ["20260628165547378908_0c84b63c"], 
"citations": [1]}, {"id": "20260628165550509892_bfddbac9", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "Hudler’s article describes the Ring of Nestor as one of several Aegean 
glyptic pieces long regarded as doubtful (gemmae dubitandae) because of unclear 
provenance and suspicions of modern production, and discusses the methodological
issues arising from such contested artifacts.", "source_ids": 
["20260628165547432051_5c281105"], "citations": [2]}, {"id": 
"20260628165550588020_07c5c9bb", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Hudler indicates 
that recent technological examinations, iconographic comparisons, and archival 
research have clarified the status of several gemmae dubitandae, including the 
Ring of Nestor, by evaluating fabrication techniques, patterns of wear, 
parallels with securely excavated rings, and historical documentation.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628165547432051_5c281105"], "citations": [2]}, {"id": 
"20260628165550664976_459f910c", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Ashmolean 
publication introduces the Ring of Nestor as a gold signet ring associated with 
a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals from Thisbê, Boeotia, 
and notes that Arthur Evans was the first archaeologist to describe it in 
detail.", "source_ids": ["20260628165547489489_3aa35dea"], "citations": [3]}, 
{"id": "20260628165550748866_fc10f3a2", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
Ashmolean text remarks that controversy over the Ring of Nestor’s authenticity 
began soon after its discovery and that later scholars challenged Evans’s 
acceptance of the ring as genuine.", "source_ids": 
["20260628165547489489_3aa35dea"], "citations": [3]}, {"id": 
"20260628165550826997_ed7b86b4", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Evans’s 1925 
monograph states that a treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals, including 
the Ring of Nestor, was found near Thisbê in Boeotia in 1924 and that this 
sepulchral assemblage was later offered for acquisition to the National 
Archaeological Museum in Athens.", "source_ids": 
["20260628165547530200_7c81ec08"], "citations": [4]}, {"id": 
"20260628165550902719_ae95a771", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Within his 
description of the Thisbê treasure, Evans singles out the Ring of Nestor as an 
unusually large gold signet ring with a complex engraved scene occupying much of
its bezel, distinguishing it from other rings in the hoard.", "source_ids": 
["20260628165547530200_7c81ec08"], "citations": [4]}, {"id": 
"20260628165550979926_67ff59fc", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Evans interprets 
the imagery on the Ring of Nestor, including human figures, boats, altars, 
sacred trees, and related motifs, as depicting aspects of a Minoan after-world 
and the soul’s journey, and links these to comparable scenes in Cretan glyptic 
and Egyptian iconography.", "source_ids": ["20260628165547530200_7c81ec08"], 
"citations": [4]}, {"id": "20260628165551058396_51a606e0", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "In arguing for authenticity, Evans compares the technical execution and
wear of the Ring of Nestor with securely excavated Late Bronze Age rings from 
Knossos and Mycenae and concludes that the Nestor ring is a genuine Late 
Helladic piece strongly influenced by Minoan artistic traditions.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628165547530200_7c81ec08"], "citations": [4]}, {"id": 
"20260628165551134267_89910010", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Journal of 
Ancient Egyptian Interconnections article explicitly notes that the so-called 
Nestor ring was found in 1924 and highlights that Evans accepted it as authentic
while many other scholars labeled it a forgery.", "source_ids": 
["20260628165547573757_a597c137"], "citations": [5]}, {"id": 
"20260628165551216888_041cea07", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The same article 
examines the ring’s engraved motifs—boats, human figures, altars or structures, 
and trees—in light of Egyptian funerary iconography to assess whether these 
elements plausibly reflect a Late Bronze Age Aegean object or instead suggest a 
modern pseudo-Minoan creation.", "source_ids": 
["20260628165547573757_a597c137"], "citations": [5]}, {"id": 
"20260628165551304238_4c26e5a3", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Metropolitan 
Museum’s Perspectives essay reports that Nanno Marinatos argued the Ring of 
Minos and the Ring of Nestor were forgeries produced by Émile Gilliéron fils, a 
restorer and copyist known for creating reconstructions of Minoan art.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628165547624681_188bb93a"], "citations": [6]}, {"id": 
"20260628165551382414_c4fa535a", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Using the case of 
the Ring of Nestor, the Met essay illustrates how objects suspected of being 
forgeries can influence interpretations of Minoan and Mycenaean art and 
underscores the need to reassess provenance and technical features of such 
artifacts in museum collections.", "source_ids": 
["20260628165547624681_188bb93a"], "citations": [6]}, {"id": 
"20260628165551469252_70d5ac8f", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Childe’s 1925 
Nature review characterizes Evans’s \"The ring of Nestor\" as an important study
that uses the ring and the Thisbê treasure to shed light on Minoan afterlife 
beliefs and the development of Aegean glyptic art, indicating that 
contemporaneous scholars considered the ring materially and interpretively 
significant.", "source_ids": ["20260628165547666258_95dbd9bd"], "citations": 
[7]}, {"id": "20260628165551543658_1023f59e", "type": "Evidence", "text": "In 
\"THE MINOAN WORLD,\" Evans discusses Boeotian gold beads and related finds as 
comparable to the Thisbê series of gold signet rings and bead seals and uses 
these comparisons to argue for technical and chronological links between Minoan 
Cretan and mainland Greek artifacts.", "source_ids": 
["20260628165547707942_eb3f42c9"], "citations": [8]}, {"id": 
"20260628165551623776_1cde6b59", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The HathiTrust 
catalog entry records Arthur Evans as the author of \"The ring of Nestor. A 
glimpse into the Minoan after-world, and a sepulchral treasure of gold 
signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia\" and dates the publication to 
1925, confirming its bibliographic details as an institutional record.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628165547747713_7844915d"], "citations": [9]}, {"id": 
"20260628165749888814_ef6fcafd", "type": "Claim", "text": "The Ring of Nestor is
a large gold Aegean signet ring, notable for its unusually big bezel and complex
engraved scene, and is part of a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and 
bead seals reportedly found near Thisbê in Boeotia, Greece, in 1924 and later 
offered to the National Archaeological Museum in Athens.", "source_ids": 
["20260628165547378908_0c84b63c", "20260628165547489489_3aa35dea", 
"20260628165547530200_7c81ec08"], "citations": [1, 3, 4]}, {"id": 
"20260628165750343675_d5519d32", "type": "Claim", "text": "Modern scholarly 
synthesis describes the Ring of Nestor as measuring approximately 3.29 cm by 
2.18 cm, making it one of the largest known Aegean gold signet rings (about the 
fifth largest in the corpus).", "source_ids": ["20260628165547378908_0c84b63c"],
"citations": [1]}, {"id": "20260628165750458918_eb08b7a0", "type": "Claim", 
"text": "The Ring of Nestor bears elaborate multi-register iconography, 
including human figures, boats, altars or structures, trees, and related motifs;
Evans and later scholars situate these scenes within Minoan and Mycenaean 
glyptic art and compare them with Egyptian funerary iconography, often 
interpreting the imagery in relation to conceptions of the afterlife and the 
soul’s journey.", "source_ids": ["20260628165547378908_0c84b63c", 
"20260628165547530200_7c81ec08", "20260628165547573757_a597c137", 
"20260628165547666258_95dbd9bd"], "citations": [1, 4, 5, 7]}, {"id": 
"20260628165750703821_c5498cb3", "type": "Claim", "text": "Arthur Evans, in his 
1925 monograph and later lecture, accepted the Ring of Nestor as an authentic 
Late Helladic (Late Bronze Age) artifact strongly influenced by Minoan artistic 
traditions, arguing this on the basis of its technical execution, wear, and 
parallels with securely excavated rings from Knossos and Mycenae.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628165547530200_7c81ec08", 
"20260628165547707942_eb3f42c9"], "citations": [4, 8]}, {"id": 
"20260628165750856452_dc4912a5", "type": "Claim", "text": "Soon after its 
reported discovery in 1924, the Ring of Nestor became a contested artifact: 
while Evans and some early reviewers treated it as an important, authentic piece
of Late Bronze Age Aegean glyptic art, many other scholars classified it among 
gemmae dubitandae and suspected it to be a modern forgery, in part because of 
unclear provenance and concerns about manufacture.", "source_ids": 
["20260628165547432051_5c281105", "20260628165547489489_3aa35dea", 
"20260628165547573757_a597c137", "20260628165547666258_95dbd9bd"], "citations": 
[2, 3, 5, 7]}, {"id": "20260628165751148352_1d047844", "type": "Claim", "text": 
"Nanno Marinatos has argued that the Ring of Nestor, along with the Ring of 
Minos, is a modern forgery produced by Émile Gilliéron fils, a restorer and 
copyist known for reconstructions of Minoan art.", "source_ids": 
["20260628165547624681_188bb93a"], "citations": [6]}, {"id": 
"20260628165751252314_583fba05", "type": "Claim", "text": "Recent scholarship, 
particularly Vlachopoulos and Hudler, reports that new technological 
examinations, iconographic comparisons, and archival research have re-evaluated 
the Ring of Nestor and other gemmae dubitandae; these studies now tend to 
support the ring’s authenticity as a Late Helladic Bronze Age artifact, though 
debate has not been fully resolved.", "source_ids": 
["20260628165547378908_0c84b63c", "20260628165547432051_5c281105"], "citations":
[1, 2]}, {"id": "20260628165751432472_a58d5a08", "type": "Claim", "text": "The 
Ring of Nestor has served as a notable case study in both early and modern 
discussions of Minoan and Mycenaean art and religion: Evans and contemporaries 
used it to explore Aegean beliefs about the afterlife and the development of 
glyptic art, while recent institutional and scholarly publications use its 
contested authenticity to illustrate methodological issues about provenance, 
forgery, and museum collections.", "source_ids": 
["20260628165547432051_5c281105", "20260628165547624681_188bb93a", 
"20260628165547666258_95dbd9bd"], "citations": [2, 6, 7]}, {"id": 
"20260628165751654733_dc6b0534", "type": "Claim", "text": "Arthur Evans’s 
monograph \"The ring of Nestor. A glimpse into the Minoan after‑world, and a 
sepulchral treasure of gold signet‑rings and bead‑seals from Thisbê, Boeotia\" 
was published in 1925 and is a primary early study of the Ring of Nestor and its
reported discovery context.", "source_ids": ["20260628165547747713_7844915d"], 
"citations": [9]}], "offset": 0, "limit": 50, "total": 27, "next_offset": null}
