{"query": {"id": "20260628165353567371_4e5b3c74", "handle": "q1", "text": "“Ring
of Nestor” archaeological ring scholarly article", "importance": "supporting"}, 
"items": [{"id": "20260628165720921183_1c40c224", "type": "Evidence", "text": 
"Evans’s work is structured in two main parts: the first, titled \"Sepulchral 
treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia\" (pp. 1–42), 
describes the treasure’s discovery and funerary context at Thisbê; the second, 
titled \"A glimpse into the Minoan after-world\" (pp. 43–75), focuses on the 
religious and mythological imagery of the rings, including the Ring of Nestor.",
"source_ids": ["20260628165717458010_62fdb26f"], "citations": [10]}, {"id": 
"20260628165721025618_b93cc273", "type": "Evidence", "text": "In his catalogue 
of the treasure’s rings, Evans lists for each item the weight in grams, the 
dimensions of the bezel (e.g., \"its bezel is 28 × 17 mm\"), and the internal 
diameter of the hoop, using these measurements to infer whether a ring was 
likely worn by a man or a woman.", "source_ids": 
["20260628165717458010_62fdb26f"], "citations": [10]}, {"id": 
"20260628165721124264_e3be6c6b", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Evans describes 
the central motif on the Ring of Nestor as a tree flanked by a guardian animal, 
which he variously characterizes as a \"watch-dog\" or a \"dragon\", situated 
within a scene including seated female figures, altars with incurved sides, and 
additional ritual participants.", "source_ids": 
["20260628165717458010_62fdb26f"], "citations": [10]}, {"id": 
"20260628165721229731_8cc545db", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Evans explicitly 
links the imagery of the Ring of Nestor to a Minoan conception of the afterlife,
referring to the ring as offering a \"glimpse into the Minoan after-world\" and 
situating its scene in the context of Minoan religious beliefs and funerary 
ideology.", "source_ids": ["20260628165717458010_62fdb26f"], "citations": [10]},
{"id": "20260628165721349899_25e2f64a", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
digitized metadata for Evans’s publication list subject keywords such as 
\"Nestor\", \"Ring\", \"Minoische Kultur\" and \"Thísvi\", indicating the 
association of the Ring of Nestor with Minoan culture and the Boeotian site of 
Thisbê.", "source_ids": ["20260628165717458010_62fdb26f"], "citations": [10]}, 
{"id": "20260628165721557397_b947f6f7", "type": "Evidence", "text": 
"Vlachopoulos reports that \"the Ring of Nestor\" measures 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm 
and notes that this makes it the fifth largest signet ring known from the Aegean
corpus.", "source_ids": ["20260628165547378908_0c84b63c"], "citations": [1]}, 
{"id": "20260628165721695322_bc7a918d", "type": "Evidence", "text": "In 
summarizing his argument, Vlachopoulos states that the goldworking techniques 
and micro-carving details observed on the Ring of Nestor match those seen on 
other Late Bronze Age gold rings whose authenticity is undoubted, thereby 
\"proving\" or strongly supporting its authenticity.", "source_ids": 
["20260628165547378908_0c84b63c"], "citations": [1]}, {"id": 
"20260628165721789682_d13c33a6", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Vlachopoulos 
explicitly identifies the animal by the central tree on the ring as a scorpion 
and discusses the scorpion’s apotropaic function, citing parallels from Bronze 
Age Aegean glyptic and other media where scorpions appear as protective or 
aggressive creatures.", "source_ids": ["20260628165547378908_0c84b63c"], 
"citations": [1]}, {"id": "20260628165721882425_b910e99f", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The article interprets the scene on the Ring of Nestor as depicting a 
scorpion attacking or killing a dragon in front of a world tree, framed by 
altars and ritual figures, which the author reads as a narrative about the 
destruction of a monstrous creature in a sacred context.", "source_ids": 
["20260628165547378908_0c84b63c"], "citations": [1]}, {"id": 
"20260628165721968584_56679571", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Eliopoulos’s 
article highlights the presence of incurved altars on the Ring of Nestor and 
compares them to similar incurved altar motifs on other Aegean artifacts, 
arguing that these visual parallels bolster the case that the ring’s imagery 
belongs to the same iconographic repertoire.", "source_ids": 
["20260628165717528938_abc29ed2"], "citations": [11]}, {"id": 
"20260628165722055303_7ecf85ae", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Eliopoulos notes 
that debates over the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos have persisted for 
generations, with both rings frequently cited as examples of possible forgeries 
in discussions about Aegean glyptic.", "source_ids": 
["20260628165717528938_abc29ed2"], "citations": [11]}, {"id": 
"20260628165722149022_7e590c42", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Wiley Online 
Library abstract states: \"Here new technical observations are presented which 
