Childe\x00b4s review in Nature, praised the work as an important investigation 
that used the ring to illuminate Minoan beliefs about the afterlife and the 
development of Aegean glyptic art.[7]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628165547747713_7844915d', '20260628165717458010_62fdb26f', 
'20260628165717657504_a3c49ae4', '20260628165717688923_b35d5a1f', 
'20260628165547666258_95dbd9bd'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'In subsequent 
decades, the Ring of Nestor has frequently appeared in discussions of Aegean 
glyptic, Minoan and Mycenaean religion, and the problem of forgery in 
archaeology. Technical and iconographic studies by Vlachopoulos, Eliopoulos, and
others have used the ring to reassess Aegean iconography and to argue for or 
against its authenticity on stylistic and technological grounds.[1][11][12] 
Hudler treats it as a central example of gemmae dubitandae and uses its 
contested status to examine how digital dissemination and improved documentation
can change debates about authenticity.[2] Institutional essays, such as the 
Metropolitan Museum of Art\x00b4s Perspectives piece on historic images of the 
Greek Bronze Age, invoke the Ring of Nestor to illustrate how objects suspected 
of being forgeries have shaped scholarly narratives and how later reassessments 
can revise understandings of museum collections.[6]', 'source_ids': 
