gold signet rings and bead seals near Thisb\x00e in Boeotia, central 
Greece.[1][4][5][10] Arthur J. Evans\x00b4s 1925 study presented the ring as an 
authentic Late Helladic (Late Bronze Age) object within a Cretan\x00f3Mycenaean 
tradition, dated broadly to the 16th\x00f315th centuries BCE and interpreted as 
offering a "glimpse into the Minoan after-world".[4][10] Subsequent scholarship 
has treated the Ring of Nestor as a paradigmatic "gemma dubitanda" (doubtful 
object): some studies, including recent technical and iconographic analyses, 
argue that its manufacture and imagery are consistent with Late Bronze Age 
Aegean glyptic art, while others describe it as a "Pseudo-Minoan" ring and 
propose that it is a modern forgery inspired by Egyptian 
iconography.[1][2][5][12] Debates over its authenticity and iconography have 
