rings and bead seals reportedly discovered near Thisb\x00e (Th\x00edsvi) in 
Boeotia in 1924.[4][10] Evans describes the find as a funerary assemblage and 
notes that the treasure was subsequently offered for acquisition to the National
Archaeological Museum in Athens, situating the ring within a mortuary context on
the Greek mainland rather than in Crete.[4][10] Later summaries repeat that the 
ring was found in 1924 and emphasize that its archaeological properties and find
circumstances are not documented to modern standards, contributing to ongoing 
uncertainty about its provenance.[5][10]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628165547530200_7c81ec08', '20260628165717458010_62fdb26f', 
'20260628165547573757_a597c137'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Evans\x00b4s 
monograph is organized in two parts: a detailed description of the "sepulchral 
treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisb\x00e, Boeotia" and a 
second section titled "A glimpse into the Minoan after-world", in which he 
analyzes the religious and mythological imagery of the rings, including the Ring
of Nestor.[10] The publication\x00b4s metadata and later bibliographic records 
list subject keywords such as "Minoische Kultur" and "Th\x00edsvi", reflecting 
its association both with Minoan culture and with the Boeotian site of 
Thisb\x00e.[9][10][13][14] Early reviewers, such as V. Gordon Childe in Nature, 
considered the study an important contribution to understanding Minoan afterlife
beliefs and Aegean glyptic, indicating that contemporaneous archaeologists took 
the reported discovery and contextual interpretation seriously.[7]', 
