'Description', 'section_type': 'section', 'blocks': [{'text': 'The Ring of 
Nestor is described in modern scholarship as a very large Aegean gold signet 
ring with an oval bezel measuring approximately 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm, making it 
one of the largest known examples in the corpus of Aegean signet rings, ranked 
around fifth in size.[1][10] Evans\x00b4s catalogue of the Thisb\x00e treasure 
lists measurements such as bezel dimensions and internal hoop diameter for each 
ring, which he uses to infer potential wearers, and the Ring of Nestor stands 
out within this series for its substantial dimensions and elaborate 
engraving.[10]', 'source_ids': ['20260628165547378908_0c84b63c', 
'20260628165717458010_62fdb26f'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The 
ring\x00b4s bezel carries a complex multi-figure scene arranged in several 
registers. Evans describes a central tree flanked by a guardian animal he terms 
a watch-dog or dragon, accompanied by seated female or possibly divine figures, 
altars with incurved sides, additional ritual participants, and related symbolic
motifs.[4][10] Other elements of the composition include boats, structures 
interpreted as altars or shrines, and further trees, creating a densely 
populated image that combines terrestrial, nautical, and sacral 
components.[4][5] A drawing of the bezel made by \x00c9mile Gilli\x00e9ron fils 
and first published by Evans in 1925 illustrates these features and has been 
widely reproduced in later studies.[10][15]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628165547530200_7c81ec08', '20260628165717458010_62fdb26f', 
'20260628165547573757_a597c137', '20260628165717728740_4dd211c5'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}], 'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260628165723411745_13e78f94']}, 
{'title': 'Iconography and interpretation', 'section_type': 'section', 'blocks':
[{'text': 'Evans interpreted the imagery on the Ring of Nestor within a 
Minoan\x00f3Mycenaean religious framework, describing it as a vision of the 
afterlife and the soul\x00b4s journey.[4][10] He linked the boats, altars with 
incurved sides, seated female figures, sacred tree, and guardian animal to 
motifs in Cretan glyptic and Egyptian funerary iconography, arguing that the 
ring offered a glimpse into a "Minoan after-world" and reflected beliefs about 
the fate of the dead.[4][10] Childe\x00b4s review emphasized that Evans used the
ring and the Thisb\x00e treasure to explore Minoan afterlife conceptions and the
development of Aegean glyptic art, showing that these interpretive themes were 
influential in contemporaneous scholarship.[7]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628165547530200_7c81ec08', '20260628165717458010_62fdb26f', 
'20260628165547666258_95dbd9bd'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Later 
iconographic studies have revisited specific elements of the scene. Vlachopoulos
identifies the animal beside the central tree not as a dog or dragon but as a 
scorpion, arguing that scorpions serve an apotropaic function in Bronze Age 
Aegean imagery and that the scene depicts a scorpion attacking or killing a 
dragon in front of a world tree, framed by altars and ritual figures.[1] By 
comparing the scorpion motif and other features with securely provenanced Aegean
glyptic, he contends that the ring\x00b4s imagery belongs to a Late Bronze Age 
iconographic repertoire.[1]', 'source_ids': ['20260628165547378908_0c84b63c'], 
'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Eliopoulos\x00b4s study of the ring\x00b4s 
iconography focuses on the incurved altars and related motifs, noting close 
parallels between these elements and those on other accepted Aegean 
artifacts.[11] He argues that such visual similarities support the view that the
Ring of Nestor participates in the same Late Bronze Age Aegean sacral 
iconography, while acknowledging that certain stylistic aspects may also 
resonate with early twentieth-century aesthetic currents.[11] The ring\x00b4s 
imagery has thus been used to discuss both continuity within Aegean religious 
art and the possibility of later reinterpretation or fabrication drawing on 
established motifs.[1][5][11]', 'source_ids': ['20260628165717528938_abc29ed2', 
'20260628165547378908_0c84b63c', '20260628165547573757_a597c137'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The Journal of Ancient Egyptian Interconnections article
treats the Ring of Nestor as a "Pseudo-Minoan" object and examines its boats, 
human figures, altars or structures, trees, and other motifs in detail against 
Egyptian funerary iconography.[5] The author argues that the close resemblance 
of several components to Egyptian visual conventions, combined with issues of 
provenance, favors interpreting the ring as a modern creation inspired by 
Egyptian models rather than as an authentic Late Bronze Age Aegean artifact.[5] 
This approach contrasts with interpretations that emphasize Aegean parallels and
highlights how differing assessments of iconographic sources inform wider 
debates about authenticity and cultural attribution.[1][5]', 'source_ids': 
